// pfodWeb NOTE: dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-12.
// Board: Valetron Systems VALTRACK-V4-MFW-ESP32-C3, a real commercial
// "mobile field worker" 4G-LTE/2G device (ESP32-C3FH4 + SIMCom
// A7672-series cellular/GNSS modem + accelerometer (MMA8652/854-family per
// firmware, LIS3DH on the sibling VTS variant) + charger/PMIC circuitry, in
// an IP67 enclosure). Sibling board VALTRACK_V4_VTS_ESP32_C3 (vehicle
// tracking system variant) shares byte-identical GPIO_* declarations in its
// own pins_arduino.h but is a different physical product, audited
// separately.
//
// Sources consulted:
//  - https://github.com/ValetronSystems/VALTRACK-V4-ESP32-C3 (official
//    vendor ESP-IDF firmware repo for both VTS and MFW designs, fetched and
//    read directly this session - main/main.c, main/ADC.c, main/FrontPanel.c
//    + .h, main/MotionSensor.h, main/include/ADC.h):
//      * main.c ~L2597/2610/2630/2642: InitGPIO() configures
//        GPIO_PWRKEY_GSM_ENABLE_PIN_SEL and GPIO_TPS_ENABLE_PIN_SEL as
//        OUTPUT, and a separate GPIO_INPUT_PIN_SEL group (comment "INT1 and
//        SOS") as INPUT - directly confirms GPIO_PWRKEY(7)/GPIO_GSM_ENABLE(10)
//        /GPIO_TPS_ENABLE(4) are dedicated outputs and GPIO_INT1(3)/GPIO_SOS(9)
//        are dedicated inputs, not general-purpose.
//      * main.c ~L1072-1105: EnableGSM()/DisableGSM() toggle GPIO_GSM_ENABLE;
//        a PWRKEY pulse routine toggles GPIO_PWRKEY (~L2277-2280, L6279-6281)
//        - the standard SIMCom-modem power-on sequence, confirms both lines
//        are the cellular modem's power control pins.
//      * main.c ~L2651-2655: gpio_hold_dis(GPIO_TPS_ENABLE) /
//        gpio_hold_dis(GPIO_GSM_ENABLE) plus gpio_hold_en() calls at
//        ~L5992-5993 during deep-sleep entry - RTC-domain pin-hold behaviour
//        used specifically to keep a power-enable line steady across sleep,
//        confirming GPIO_TPS_ENABLE is a dedicated PMIC/regulator enable
//        line (name matches a TI TPS-series regulator), not a general pin.
//      * main.c ~L2712/6695: INT1 = gpio_get_level(GPIO_INT1) - read inside
//        the motion-sensor status path; MotionSensor.h shows this firmware
//        supports either LIS3DH (VTS, `#ifdef VALTRACK_V4_VTS`) or an
//        MMA8652/854-family accelerometer (MFW, the #else branch) at I2C
//        address 0x1D - confirms GPIO_INT1 is this board's onboard
//        accelerometer interrupt pin.
//      * main.c ~L1424 onward and L2727/3726/4500/4790/5496/6645/6763:
//        SOS = gpio_get_level(GPIO_SOS), feeding SOSActivated - confirms
//        GPIO_SOS is a genuine physical panic-button input, actively
//        polled throughout the main loop.
//      * ADC.c: EXAMPLE_ADC1_CHAN0 = ADC_CHANNEL_2 (ESP32-C3 ADC1 channel 2
//        = GPIO2, matching GPIO_ANALOG_IN); the reading is stored in a
//        variable literally named `ADCBatteryVoltage`, scaled by a
//        `DIVIDER_FACTOR` and `ADC_OFFSET` - directly confirms GPIO2/
//        GPIO_ANALOG_IN is a battery-voltage-sense input behind a resistor
//        divider, not a generic/unknown analog pin. This is firmware-
//        source-confirmed, not a name-based guess.
//      * FrontPanel.c/.h + led_strip_rmt_ws2812.c/led_strip_encoder.c:
//        implements a WS2812/NeoPixel-protocol RGB LED strip (3 logical
//        LEDs: Battery/Network/Location, each R/G/B) driven via the ESP-IDF
//        RMT peripheral, matching GPIO_LED_SIGNAL_PIN_SEL's use in
//        InitGPIO() (configured as a plain OUTPUT, then handed to the RMT/
//        WS2812 encoder) - confirms GPIO_LED_SIGNAL(8) is a dedicated
//        addressable-RGB-LED data line, not a simple digital LED.
//      * GPIO_CHG_IN (GPIO4, same value as GPIO_TPS_ENABLE): grepped the
//        entire main.c - every reference to GPIO_CHG_IN is inside a fully
//        commented-out block (~L6530-6545, "// #define GPIO_CHG_IN 4" /
//        "//gpio_set_level(GPIO_CHG_IN, 0)"). The live charging-status read
//        (`ChargingStatus = gpio_get_level(GPIO_CHARGER_PIN)`, MFW-only
//        branch `#ifndef VALTRACK_V4_VTS` at ~L2714/6269) uses a DIFFERENT,
//        differently-named macro (GPIO_CHARGER_PIN) whose value is not
//        defined anywhere in the public repo (likely a private/per-revision
//        config header not committed). Conclusion: GPIO_TPS_ENABLE is the
//        real, actively-used declaration for GPIO4 (confirmed live code
//        use); GPIO_CHG_IN is dead/vestigial copy-paste code, per the task's
//        own suspicion. Regardless of which name is "real," GPIO4 is
//        dedicated PMIC/power-control hardware either way, so both
//        declarations are excluded together.
//      * TX1/RX1 (GPIO0/GPIO1): not itself in the originally-flagged pin
//        list, but SCI.c/SCI.h ("Serial Communication Interface") is this
//        firmware's AT-command driver talking to the cellular modem over a
//        second UART, distinct from the ESP32's main UART0 (TX=21/RX=20,
//        used for the pfod Serial connection) - confirmed via
//        code/file-name inspection during this session. Excluded as a
//        dedicated modem UART, matching the same finding independently made
//        for the sibling VTS board.
//  - https://docs.valetron.com/wiki/products/valtrack-v4-vts-esp32-c3/firmware
//    Vendor wiki (VTS page, but describes the shared V4 hardware platform):
//    confirms LIS3DH accelerometer (I2C), SIM7600/A7672-family modem via
//    Serial1 AT commands, GPIO_TPS_ENABLE(4)/GPIO_CHG_IN(4 - both on pin 4),
//    GPIO_SOS(9) physical SOS button, GPIO_LED_SIGNAL(8) addressable
//    NeoPixel strip (battery/network/location status).
//  - https://docs.valetron.com/wiki/products/valtrack-v4-vts-esp32-c3/specifications
//    Vendor hardware spec page: LIS3DH accelerometer, A7672S/E/SA/SIM7672G/NA
//    regional modem variants (4G-LTE-CAT1/2G + inbuilt GNSS), MP2617 Li-ion
//    charger IC, "Aux Inputs: None" / "Aux Outputs: None" - i.e. no
//    documented general-purpose/exposed auxiliary I/O header on this
//    product family, supporting the I2C-bus-is-internal-only conclusion
//    below (this page describes the VTS SKU; MFW hardware is understood to
//    share the same core platform per the shared firmware repo, but no
//    MFW-specific spec page was found - see notes.txt).
//  - https://www.espboards.dev/esp32/valtrack-v4-mfw-esp32-c3/ - independent
//    pinout table for THIS specific board (MFW): corroborates the same
//    GPIO table already present in this board's own pins_arduino.h
//    (GPIO_TPS_ENABLE/GPIO_CHG_IN on 4, GPIO_PWRKEY on 7, GPIO_GSM_ENABLE
//    on 10, GPIO_SOS on 9, GPIO_LED_SIGNAL on 8, GPIO_IIC_DATA/CLOCK on
//    5/6) - treated as confirming-but-not-fully-independent since it likely
//    derives from the same vendor-submitted Arduino core board entry.
//  - https://hackaday.io/project/190659-esp32-c3-powered-vehicle-gps-tracker-valtrack-v4
//    and https://www.cnx-software.com/2025/06/14/valtrack-v4-programmable-4g-lte-gps-vehicle-tracker-with-esp32-c3-bluetooth-wi-fi-soc/
//    General product background: confirms this is a real, IP67-enclosed,
//    open-source-firmware commercial tracker product family, not a hobbyist
//    dev board - supports treating undocumented pins conservatively (sealed
//    enclosure, no general-purpose header) rather than assuming exposure.
//
// Per-pin decisions (all EXCLUDED pins also had their static const alias,
// AND every blanket-identity A<n>/D<n>/SPI-boilerplate alias of the same
// GPIO number, deleted from this board's pins_arduino.h - see that file's
// own pfodWeb NOTE comment):
//
// GPIO0/GPIO1 (TX1/RX1): EXCLUDED. Second UART (SCI.c "Serial Communication
// Interface" AT-command driver), distinct from the main UART0 TX/RX
// (GPIO21/20) used for the pfod Serial connection. Firmware-source- and
// vendor-repo-corroborated.
//
// GPIO3 (GPIO_INT1): EXCLUDED. Firmware-confirmed accelerometer interrupt
// pin (MotionSensor.h shows LIS3DH-or-MMA8652/854 selection; main.c reads
// GPIO_INT1 into a MotionStatusType). Vendor-corroborated, not a name guess.
//
// GPIO4 (GPIO_TPS_ENABLE - the live/used declaration - and GPIO_CHG_IN, a
// dead/commented-out duplicate, both = 4): EXCLUDED. GPIO_TPS_ENABLE is
// firmware-confirmed as an output with deep-sleep pin-hold behaviour typical
// of a PMIC/regulator enable line. GPIO_CHG_IN is confirmed dead code (only
// appears inside a fully-commented block); the live charge-status read uses
// a differently-named, undefined-in-public-repo macro instead. Regardless,
// GPIO4 is dedicated power-management hardware either way.
//
// GPIO5/GPIO6 (GPIO_IIC_DATA/GPIO_IIC_CLOCK, also declared as the standard
// SDA/SCL aliases): EXCLUDED. Firmware-confirmed I2C bus used exclusively
// for i2c_master_init()+InitAccelerometer() (the onboard accelerometer).
// Vendor spec page documents "Aux Inputs: None"/"Aux Outputs: None" (no
// general-purpose I2C header on this product line) and the device is a
// sealed IP67 enclosure with no accessible header - no evidence of
// general-purpose/multi-drop use, unlike the playbook's default "keep I2C
// available" guidance for boards with an exposed header.
//
// GPIO7 (GPIO_PWRKEY): EXCLUDED. Firmware-confirmed modem PWRKEY pulse
// sequence (gpio_set_level toggled high-then-low, the standard SIMCom-modem
// power-on procedure). Vendor-corroborated.
//
// GPIO8 (GPIO_LED_SIGNAL): KEPT, tagged led_neopixel. Firmware-confirmed
// (FrontPanel.c/.h + led_strip_rmt_ws2812.c/led_strip_encoder.c) WS2812/
// NeoPixel-protocol addressable RGB LED strip (3 logical status LEDs:
// battery/network/location). Not the board's TX/RX/TX1/RX1 (21/20/0/1) - no
// conflict. pwm_output kept since GPIO8 supports the chip's LEDC peripheral
// at the silicon level (matches this repo's existing led_neopixel
// precedent, e.g. waveshare_esp32_s3_zero GPIO21, and the sibling VTS
// board's same decision).
//
// GPIO9 (GPIO_SOS): KEPT, tagged button. Firmware-confirmed: actively
// polled throughout main.c's main loop (SOS = gpio_get_level(GPIO_SOS),
// feeding a SOSActivated flag), configured as a dedicated INPUT alongside
// GPIO_INT1 in InitGPIO(). Vendor wiki corroborates a physical SOS button.
// Not the board's TX/RX/TX1/RX1 (21/20/0/1) - no conflict.
//
// GPIO10 (GPIO_GSM_ENABLE): EXCLUDED. Firmware-confirmed
// EnableGSM()/DisableGSM() toggle this pin, configured as OUTPUT alongside
// GPIO_PWRKEY, with deep-sleep pin-hold calls - the modem's power-gate
// enable line. Vendor-corroborated.
//
// GPIO2 (GPIO_ANALOG_IN): KEPT, capabilities left at the chip-level default
// (analog_input/digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output - not a button/LED
// tag, per playbook's analog-sensor KEEP category which doesn't restrict
// the capability set). Firmware-source-confirmed via ADC.c: reads
// ADC_CHANNEL_2 (= GPIO2 on ESP32-C3 ADC1) into a variable literally named
// `ADCBatteryVoltage`, scaled by a `DIVIDER_FACTOR`/`ADC_OFFSET` - this is a
// battery-voltage-sense input behind a resistor divider, confirmed from
// real firmware source (stronger evidence than a name-based guess).
//
// GPIO11: not aliased anywhere in this board's pins_arduino.h - left at the
// chip-level default, unlabeled, no note needed.
{
  "boardName": "ESP32C3",
  "displayName": "ESP32-C3 Dev Module",
  "family": "esp32",
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },
  "dac": {},
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" },
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],
  "connections": ["serial", "ble", "tcp", "http"],
  "pinNotes": {
    "2": "Must be HIGH for normal boot",
    "8": "Must be HIGH or floating on reboot",
    "9": "Must be HIGH for normal boot"
  },
  "chipGpios": {
    "2":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"], "note": "GPIO_ANALOG_IN in vendor pins_arduino.h - firmware source (ADC.c) confirms this reads battery voltage via a resistor divider (variable ADCBatteryVoltage, DIVIDER_FACTOR, ADC_OFFSET). See boardsDetails notes.txt." },
    "8":  { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel", "pwm_output"] },
    "9":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"] },
    "11": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] }
  }
}
